Image SXM history        v1.60        ( Apr 96 - Dec 96 )____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ∑8    ( 18 Dec 96 )Added features: • Five new items have been added to the SPM/Calibration submenu. 'Calibrate Distortion' calibrates the geometric distortion caused by viewing a surface at an angle to the normal (seen in low-magnification SEM images of tilted surfaces or images of a viewing screen of a TEM). This correction can be applied subsequently to other images using the 'Apply Correction' command. Calibration data can be saved to a specified file or, if the option key is pressed, the default file 'Image SXM Geom Dist Calib' in the Preferences folder (which will be read automatically when Image SXM initialises). Data can be loaded from either a text file or from a text window. Requested by an Image SXM user. • Four new text translation items have been added to the SPM/Parameters submenu. These enable parameter files (or any other text files) to be translated between Mac and Unix or PC formats. • A new 'Differentiate' item has been added to the Process menu. The local gradient (top-left to bottom-right) is calculated for each pixel and scaled such that zero gradient is in the middle of the LUT. If the option key is pressed then the scale is expanded by a factor of x3. • A new 'Set Z Scale...' item has been added to the SPM/Calibrate submenu which allows the Z scale of images to be set by direct manual input of the Z values of the black and white pixels. Requested by an Image SXM user. • A new 'Slope Histogram...' item has been added to the Analyze menu. This produces a 2d histogram of the x and y slopes of the pixels in a rectangular region of interest (ROI). Requested by an Image SXM user. • Image SXM can now read Digital Instruments NanoScope III EC (electrochemical) STM images. • Image SXM can now read old-format Park Scientific Instruments images. Bug fixes: • Pressing cmd-Z (for 'Undo') produced the 'Z' cursor. A zoom window can be produced only if the command key is not pressed, and so the cursor should not have changed. • Fixed a bug that caused a crash if a 16-bit TIFF image could not be loaded due to lack of memory. • Omicron SPM image files use the identifying letters 'tb' for one of two distinct purposes: as a prefix it identifies a friction image (old Omicron format) and as a suffix it means 'topographic backward' (new Omicron format). As a result of this, Image SXM could confuse new-format images with old-format images. • After selecting the 'Calibrate...' item, the display of the calibration curve was incomplete. • 'Revert to Saved' ignored the 'Å' or 'nm' or 'µm' setting in the 'SPM Options' dialog box.Other changes: • A 'View History' item has been added to the Help menu. It displays this file and so provides a convenient way for the user to check on the most recent modifications to Image SXM. • When memory is low, opening an image can be quite slow as it is loaded line by line. The user is now warned of this situation and a progress bar is displayed during loading.  • Axes can now be added to a 3d Plot by checking a checkbox in the dialog box. Axes can be added or removed from an existing 3d Plot window by typing cmd-option-A. To get the perspective of the axes right with respect to the image the 3d Plot routine has been partly recoded. As a result it is about 10% slower.  • A 'Faster/Better' pair of radio buttons has been added to the 3d Plot dialog box. The 'Faster' option produces a 3d plot as previously. The 'Better' option enlarges the image, makes a 3d plot, smooths the plot and then reduces the size of the plot window slightly. The result is a higher quality 3d plot, but it takes about 4 times longer to draw and requires more free memory. Unlike for the 'Faster' option, the view angles of 3d plots created using the 'Better' option cannot be altered using the cursor keys. I would suggest that the 'Better' option is only used when you are sure of the view/shadow/magnification settings. • New-format Omicron SPM images will now load even if their parameter files are left in their original Unix format. • Support for Gatan Digital Micrograph (DigiScan) images has been improved.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ∑7    ( 27 Oct 96 )Added features: • Image SXM can now read images from the Gatan DigiScan system saved in PICT, TIFF or native Gatan format. The TIFF image loading routines have been modified to handle the 16-bit TIFF images. • Image SXM can now read images saved by the Screen Machine frame grabber made by Fast Electronics GmbH.Bug fixes: • When browsing Omicron SPM images using 'Browse Omicron...' the window title was displayed in the wrong font and size. • The text of two Balloon Help items were interchanged in the 'ReCalibrate Z...' dialog box. • In the 'Set Scale...' dialog box, changing the units from the pop-up menu would leave the display of the 'Known Distance' entry unchanged even though the scale had been changed. This was a bug in NIH Image.Other changes: • Cosmetic changes have been made to all dialog boxes. They now have a grey background and some sets of radio buttons or checkboxes have been grouped together in an attempt to clarify their function. In dialog boxes that have had Image SXM items added to the original set of NIH Image items, these have now been separated for clarity. If you prefer the original white background to all dialog boxes, uncheck the 'Use Greyscale Dialog Boxes' item in the 'Preferences' dialog box. The change will take effect immediately. • A number of modifications have been made to the browsing routines. When browsing, the dialog box that appears after the window fills with images now has 'Print' button. A 'Print All Images' checkbox, added to both the 'Browse Images' and 'Browse Omicron' dialog boxes, will print all images browsed without bringing up the usual 'Print' dialog box. In addition, 'Browse Images' can be set to skip a specified number of images (which defaults to the number browsed previously). • The 'Get Info' display can be brought up automatically whenever an SXM image is opened or selected. This option is controlled from a checkbox in the 'SXM Options' dialog box. • When Image SXM opens a text file bigger than 32 kbytes, it tests to see if it is actually an SXM image file with the file type set incorrectly. If so, then it loads the image. If not, then it treats it as a text file. • If a line profile is too short to be used for the calculation of step heights (< 20 pixels), the user is informed. • The action required to create a Zoom window has been changed from Tab-click to z-click. The Zoom window, introduced in Image SXM v1.57, is a small window showing a magnified view of the region of the image around the cursor (default 2x magnification, 4x if the shift key is also pressed). The zoomed image can be temporarily frozen by pressing the caps lock key.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ∑6    ( 6 Jul 96 )Added features: • A new 'Image Correlation' item has been added to the SPM/Images submenu. It produces a plot of the correlation between the intensities of pixels in the first image in a stack and those in the second. The value of a pixel in the correlation plot indicates the number of image pixels that have a given value in the first image (X-axis) and the second image (Y-axis) at the same position in each image. It is functionally the same as the 'image_correlation' macro posted on zippy.nimh.nih.gov, but is 100 times faster.Bug fixes: • The independent Z scales for each slice in a stack of SPM images were incorrect if a slice was added or deleted manually after the stack was created. A slice added to such a stack will by default have the Z scale calibration of the slice after the current slice. • The image data types of multiple images in Digital Instruments NanoScope III files are now displayed correctly by 'Get Info'. • Browsing NanoScope III files containing multiple images would leave 'phantom' windows listed in the Windows menu.  • After applying the 'Match LUTs' command the Z scale calibrations are now displayed correctly by 'Get Info'.Other changes: • The 'SXM Help...' menu item, previously the first item in the SEM and SPM menus, has been moved to the Help menu (the '?' in the menu bar). The Help window can be left open when selecting 'Windows to Stack' from the Stacks menu – it will be closed automatically and re-opened at the same page after the stack has been made. • WA Technology SPM images that have pixel aspect ratios other than unity (ie, different X and Y scales due to image processing by the acquisition software) now have the image stretched along one axis to restore the same spatial scale on both axes. This ensures that profile plots are labelled correctly. • 'MatchLUTs' now only affects images that have Z scales with the same units as that of the frontmost image. • The icons used for Image SXM and its associated files can now be reset to the standard NIH Image icons by deselecting a checkbox in the 'Preferences...' dialog box.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ∑6 T    ( 24 Jun 96 )    Test releaseAdded features: • Image SXM can now read Digital Instruments NanoScope force curves. The curves are plotted (by the same routines that are used to plot line profiles) and then copied into an image window. The size of the plot windows and the choice of displaying extending or retracting curves are set in the 'SPM Options' dialog box (select Digital Instruments from the pop-up menu). Other options are set from the 'Profile Plot Options' dialog box. This new feature was requested by an Image SXM user. • Data from WA Technology IV spectra and Digital Instruments NanoScope force curves can be exported by choosing the Plot Values button in the 'Export...' dialog box. This will work only for the IV spectrum or force curve loaded most recently. • Two new items have been added to the 'FFT' submenu to be used with diffraction images. Low-energy electron diffraction (LEED) images can be transformed into real space images using 'Inverse FFT of LEED'. The LEED image is converted into an FFT and then an inverse FFT operation is applied. The LUT of the LEED image will be inverted if necessary. Converting a LEED image into an FFT using 'Make LEED into FFT' will enable the display of frequency and period values in the Info window (as for any 'normal' FFT) but will not apply an inverse FFT. Bug fixes: • None.Other changes: • Image SXM will now attempt to load any Digital Instruments NanoScope II image file, providing that the data file type read from the header is greater than 1 and less than 10. Previously, it would only load image files of data file type 7. ____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ∑5    ( 15 Jun 96 )Added features: • A new 'Match LUTs' item has been added to the SPM/Images submenu. It changes the look-up tables of the open image windows such that they all have the same Z scale calibration (nm per grey level). This new feature was requested by an Image SXM user.Bug fixes: • Converting 'Windows to Stack' or 'Stack to Windows' would crash Image SXM if there were more than ten images. This was due to the maximum number of images in an SPM stack being set to ten. Any number of images can now be stacked, but only the first ten slices can have independent Z scales and units (see v1.59 ∑1).Other changes: • None.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ∑4    ( 12 Jun 96 )Added features: • None.Bug fixes: • As a result of modifying the Profile Plot routines for WA Technology IV spectra and profiles of FFTs, the spatial scale of an image was inadvertently altered by the profile calculation.Other changes: • Changing file types in the System Folder or Applications folder is now not permitted.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ∑3    ( 9 Jun 96 )Added features: • None.Bug fixes: • If selected via the 'SXM Preferences' dialog box, making changes in the 'LUT Options' dialog box would make Image SXM quit.  • The last image loaded from a file containing multiple images would be displayed with a greyscale LUT, regardless of the LUT of the original image.Other changes: • A JEOL WinSem image will now load even if the associated parameter file is missing or corrupt. The spatial scale will be uncalibrated.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ∑2    ( 2 Jun 96 )Added features: • None.Bug fixes: • The 'Image Math...' routines did not handle operations between FFTs correctly. This was a result of a small difference between the FFT routines in Image SXM v1.55-1.58 and those introduced into NIH Image v1.59. Some of the FFT macros, such as autocorrelation, use these routines and thus are now working OK. • Constraining line selections in FFTs is now more robust. One 'bug' still remains: Dragging the end of an existing line selection through an X or Y axis such that the line length reduces to zero momentarily will result in a one-pixel displacement of subsequent profile plots. This is a result of the 'start' and 'end' of the line selection being swapped over, and is awkward to fix. Other changes: • The latest release of the CodeWarrior compiler generates more efficient PowerPC code compared to earlier versions, resulting in slightly faster execution speeds. The speeds tabulated in the last two pages of the Help display have not yet been updated. As an indication of the improvement: On a Power Mac 8100/100, the integer benchmark has dropped from 0.8 to 0.6 s and the floating point benchmark has dropped from 0.9 to 0.8 s. • Profiles can now be averaged. Pressing 'S' when a profile is displayed will store the current profile data. The line selection can be moved and the new profile stored an unlimited number of times. Pressing 'D' will display an average of the stored profiles. The Info window displays a count of how many profiles have been stored.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ∑1    ( 19 May 96 )Added features: • None.Bug fixes: • The display of frequency and period values in the Info window for FFTs of images with no spatial calibration was incorrect. • The display precision of measurements in the Results window was incorrect.Other changes: • Plots of WA Technology IV spectra are now labelled with the filename in the top right corner.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ß4    ( 15 May 96 )    Beta releaseAdded features: • Image SXM can now read WA Technology IV spectra files v3.0-v4.04. The spectra are plotted (by the same routines that are used to plot line profiles) and then copied into an image window. Pressing the option key while the IV spectrum is loaded will smooth the data. The size of the plot windows is set in the 'SPM Options' dialog box (select WA Technology from the pop-up menu) and other options are set from the 'STS Options' dialog box (shortcut: double-click or option-click in an IV plot window). This new feature was requested by an Image SXM user.Bug fixes: • The units displayed in the 'ReCalibrate Z...' dialog box were 'Å' rather than the actual units of the current image.Other changes: • The 'Unsharp Mask' command in the Process menu now uses a custom 11x11 convolution kernel. The effect of applying this convolution is the same as for the previous implementation (which subtracted from the original image a copy of the image that had been smoothed by convolution with a Gaussian). Pressing the option key uses a 7x7 kernel (faster, but less detail of the original image is retained). Pressing the shift key uses a 15x15 kernel (slower, but more detail is retained). • The 'SXM Help...' display can now be activated using the 'help' key on Apple extended keyboards. Also, the user can move from page to page by clicking in the bottom left or bottom right corners of a page. • Opening Omicron SPM image files that contain two images (scan left -> right and right -> left) now loads both images and labels them '>>>' and '<<<', respectively. • The code that handles the various image file formats that Image SXM supports has had a Spring clean. The number of formats supported is now more than a dozen, and the code was in need of a tidy up. Chunks of code that were similar for more than one format were combined and redundant code removed. This should not have any effect on loading images, but the probability of introducing a bug in the process is not insignificant.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ß3    ( 1 May 96 )    Beta releaseAdded features: • None. Bug fixes: • If a line selection is nudged more than once to update a Profile Plot, then the line selection is redrawn on the image between each update. This ensures that the line selection is seen to move in synchronisation with the changing Profile Plot. • The 'OpenAll' checkbox in the 'Open...' dialog box now works again on SXM images (the bug was fixed in v1.59 ß2, but reappeared when the SXM routines were spliced into NIH Image v1.60).Other changes: • When browsing SXM images, the user now has the choice of displaying the images either at a fixed size (small = 64 pixels, large = 128 pixels wide) or at a fixed reduction factor (chosen from four values, as before). Borders are now drawn around each image (in black or white, depending on the background colour selected). • The compensation routines can now alter the LUT of the resulting image to cover all pixels in the image, eliminating saturated pixels. Thus, there are now three modes of operation: (a) selecting compensation (or recompensation) with no modifier keys pressed will apply the compensation and leave the LUT unchanged; (b) pressing the option key will enhance the contrast so that the image within the region of interest uses all the greys in the LUT; (c) pressing the shift key will reduce the contrast so that the entire image lies within the range of the LUT.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ß2    ( 13 Apr 96 )    Beta releaseAdded features: • None. Bug fixes: • The Profile Plot axes were not redrawn properly if the following three conditions were met: (i) an image window overlapped with the Profile Plot window, (ii) the option key was pressed to produce a profile overlay, and (iii) the line selection was dragged with the mouse rather than nudged using the arrow keys. If these conditions occur, the axes are now redrawn (causing some flicker on slower Macs). • After applying selective recompensation to an image, the next image to be loaded would have the compensation applied selectively rather than to the entire image. This bug has been fixed. • A memory leak in the compensation routines that would slowly eat up free RAM has been fixed.Other changes: • Image headers saved in all PICT and TIFF files now store the identity of the original SXM format. This allows users of more than one type of SEM, SPM or SAM system to identify the origin of a PICT or TIFF image. • The maximum number of Z values in an image has been increased to 128K. Although 16-bit images can contain no more than 64K different Z values, calculating  tilt or curvature compensation over a small selected region can yield a wider range of values if the surface is highly corrugated. • The keyboard shortcut cmd-option-D has been added for the 'Display Parameters' item of the SPM/Parameters submenu. • A new method of expanding the contrast of an image has been added. Double-clicking the LUT tool (the one above the 'A' in the Tool window) with the option key pressed puts two markers in the LUT and Histogram windows. The user can drag these markers and then select Apply LUT (cmd-L) to expand the contrast such that black and white then correspond to these markers. This option was requested by an Image SXM user. • The 'Browse Omicron...' command now recognises AFM friction images.____________________________________________________________________________________v1.60    ß1    ( 9 Apr 96 )    Beta releaseAdded features: • None. Bug fixes: • NIH Image v1.60 fixes a number of bugs present in v1.59. See the NIH Image documentation for details.Other changes: • The first items in each of the SEM and SPM menus have been renamed 'SXM Help...' and now produce a window which displays a sequence of help pages. These contain general information about Image SXM and a description of all the commands available from the SEM and SPM menus. The user can move from page to page using the '<' and '>' keys (and also the home, end, page up and page down keys). • A checkbox in the 'SPM Options' dialog box now allows the user to suppress the display of the window title suffix that indicates the compensation mode(s) that have been applied to the image. • When making a rectangular selection in a (non-FFT) image, pressing the Shift and 'F' keys constrains the selection to be a square of size equal to a power of two. This is an alternative to the 'Select Largest Square' command in the FFT submenu. • When selecting a rectangular or elliptical region of interest within an FFT image, pressing the Shift key constrains the selection to be a square or circle centred on the origin of the FFT. This makes filtering the FFT easier before doing an inverse FFT. • When the frontmost image is an FFT, the keyboard shortcut cmd-I executes an 'Inverse FFT' rather than the usual 'Get Info' command. • The Z scale calibration of old-format WA Technology STM images is now calculated using a slightly different algorithm. This should give the correct calibration for all such images, but is still largely untested.____________________________________________________________________________________